      "text": "We start with the given equation $\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x.$ Using the identity for the cosine of a difference, we rewrite the equation as:\n$$\n\\cos (2x - 3x) = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x - \\sin 2x \\sin 3x.\n$$\nThis can be further simplified to:\n$$\n\\cos (-x) = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x - \\sin 2x \\sin 3x,\n$$\nby recognizing that $\\cos (-x) = \\cos x.$ Expanding the equation using the cosine double-angle identities, we get:\n$$\n\\cos x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x - 2 \\cos 2x \\sin^2 x.\n$$\nFactoring out $\\cos x$ on the right side, we obtain:\n$$\n\\cos x = \\cos x (\\cos 2x \\cos 3x - 2 \\cos 2x \\sin^2 x).\n$$\nSince $\\cos x \\neq 0$ for distinct solutions, we can divide both sides by $\\cos x$ to get:\n$$\n1 = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x - 2 \\cos 2x \\sin^2 x.\n$$\nMultiplying through by $\\cos x$ to clear the denominators, we have:\n$$\n\\cos x = \\cos x \\cos 2x \\cos 3x - 2 \\cos^3 x \\sin^2 x.\n$$\nDividing out $\\cos x$ (which is not equal to zero), we get:\n$$\n1 = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x - 2 \\cos^3 x \\sin^2 x.\n$$\nSince both sides of the equation have $\\cos x$, we can simplify it to:\n$$\n1 = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x - 2 \\cos^3 x \\sin^2 x.\n$$\nNotice that if we divide both sides by $\\cos x,$ we obtain:\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\cos x} = 2 \\cos^2 x \\sin^2 x.\n$$\nMultiplying both sides by $\\cos x,$ we get:\n$$\n\\cos x = 2 \\cos^3 x \\sin^2 x.\n$$\nDividing both sides by $\\cos x,$ we arrive at our identity:\n$$\n1 = 2 \\cos^2 x \\sin^2 x.\n$$\nDividing both sides by $2 \\sin^2 x = \\frac{1}{\\cos x}$ and using the Pythagorean identity, we find:\n$$\n\\sin^2 x = \\frac{1}{2 \\cos^2 x}.\n$$\nSubstituting the identity $\\cos x = \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 x}$ from $\\sin x,$ we have:\n$$\n4 \\sin^2 x - 2 \\cos 2x = 1.\n$$\nSolving this step-by-step ($\\sin^2 x = \\frac{1}{2}$) gives us:\n$$\n\\sin x = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\nwhich in degrees results in:\n$$\n\\boxed{45}.\n$$",
