      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\), we can use the trigonometric identity for the cosine of the sum of two angles:\n\n\\[\n\\cos (a + b) = \\cos a \\cos b - \\sin a \\sin b\n\\]\n\nRewriting the given equation using this identity:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x - \\cos 2x \\cos 3x = 0\n\\]\n\nWe recognize that this is equivalent to:\n\n\\[\n- (\\cos 2x \\cos 3x - \\sin 2x \\sin 3x) = 0\n\\]\n\nUsing the cosine sum identity:\n\n\\[\n- \\cos (2x + 3x) = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the term inside the cosine function:\n\n\\[\n- \\cos 5x = 0\n\\]\n\nThus, we find:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 5x = 0\n\\]\n\nThe general solution for \\(\\cos \\theta = 0\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\theta = 90^\\circ + 180^\\circ k \\quad \\text{where} \\quad k \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n\\]\n\nSo, for \\(\\theta = 5x\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n5x = 90^\\circ + 180^\\circ k\n\\]\n\n\\\nSolving for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{90^\\circ + 180^\\circ k}{5}\n\\]\n\nTo find the smallest positive \\(x\\) within the first cycle \\([0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)\\), we consider \\(k = 0\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{90^\\circ}{5} = 18^\\circ\n\\]\n\nFor \\(k = 1\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{90^\\circ + 180^\\circ \\cdot 1}{5} = \\frac{90^\\circ + 180^\\circ}{5} = \\frac{270^\\circ}{5} = 54^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest positive \\(x\\) meeting the condition is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{18}\n\\]",
