      "text": "We start with the given equation in the problem:\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x.\n\\]\nRecall the trigonometric identity for the cosine of the sum or difference of angles:\n\\[\n\\cos A \\cos B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (A + B) + \\cos (A - B)].\n\\]\nIn our case, let \\( A = 2x \\) and \\( B = 3x \\). Then we can rewrite the right-hand side of our equation using this identity:\n\\[\n\\cos 2x \\cos 3x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (2x + 3x) + \\cos (2x - 3x)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 5x + \\cos (-x)].\n\\]\nSince \\(\\cos (-x) = \\cos x\\), the above simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\cos 2x \\cos 3x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 5x + \\cos x].\n\\]\nThus, the original equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 5x + \\cos x].\n\\]\nNext, we use the sum-to-product identities for sine and cosine:\n\\[\n\\sin A \\sin B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (A - B) - \\cos (A + B)].\n\\]\nApplying this identity to \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (2x - 3x) - \\cos (2x + 3x)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (-x) - \\cos 5x] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos x - \\cos 5x].\n\\]\nSo, the original equation now reads:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} [\\cos x - \\cos 5x] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 5x + \\cos x].\n\\]\nThis implies:\n\\[\n\\cos x - \\cos 5x = \\cos 5x + \\cos x.\n\\]\nSimplifying, we obtain:\n\\[\n- \\cos 5x = \\cos 5x,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n2 \\cos 5x = 0.\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n\\cos 5x = 0.\n\\]\nThe general solution for \\(\\cos y = 0\\) is \\(y = 90^\\circ + 180^\\circ k\\) for any integer \\(k\\). Applying this to \\(\\cos 5x = 0\\), we get:\n\\[\n5x = 90^\\circ + 180^\\circ k,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nx = 18^\\circ + 36^\\circ k.\n\\]\nFor the smallest positive \\(x\\), we take \\(k = 0\\), giving:\n\\[\nx = 18^\\circ.\n\\]\nTherefore, the smallest positive angle \\(x\\) that satisfies the given equation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{18}.\n\\]",
