      "text": "We know that $\\sin(A+B)=\\sin(A)\\cos(B)+\\cos(A)\\sin(B)$, so we can write $\\sin(A+B)-\\sin(A-B)=2\\sin(A)\\cos(B)$.\nThus, $\\sin(2x+3x)-2\\sin(2x)\\cos(3x)=0$.\nApplying the angle addition formula on $2x+3x$, we have $\\sin(5x)=-2\\sin(2x)\\cos(3x)$.\nWe know that $\\sin(A)=\\cos90^\\circ-A$ (Unit Circle) and $\\cos(A)=\\sin90^\\circ-A$ (Unit Circle), so we can rewrite the equation as follows:\n$-\\cos(3x)=-2\\cos(90^\\circ-2x)=2\\cos(2x)$.\nRearranging the equation, we have $\\cos(2x)+\\cos(3x)=0$.\nUsing the trigonometric identity $\\cos(A)\\cos(B)+\\sin(A)\\sin(B)=\\cos(A-B)$, we can rewrite the equation as follows:\n$\\cos(2x+3x)=0$.\nSince $\\cos(180^\\circ)=0$, we know that $2x+3x=180^\\circ+2(k)(360^\\circ)$ for some integer $k$.\nTherefore, $5x=180^\\circ + 2(k)(360^\\circ)$.\nTo find the smallest positive angle $x$, we want to minimize $5x$.\nSetting $k=1$, we find that $x=\\frac{180^\\circ + 2(360^\\circ)}{5}=\\boxed{168^\\circ}$.\nThe answer is: 168",
